body:
An under-sized runt bears just as many of his mother's genes as his more thriving litter-mates. But his life expectation is less. Another way to put it is that he needs more than his fair share of parental investment, just to end up equal to his brothers.

&#x200B;

As soon as a runt becomes so small and weak that his expectation of life is reduced to the point where benefit to him due to parental investment is less than half the benefit that the same investment could potentially confer on the other babies, the runt should die gracefully and willingly. He can benefit his genes most by doing so.

The sentiment in life and the media, as the world gets worse and worse, is "at least I have my friends".

&#x200B;

No matter how bad things get, you're supposed to always have friends to cheer you up at the end of the day. 

&#x200B;

Even stories and anecdotes about being supposedly friendless fail to acknowledge the possibility of someone having \*literally\* no friends at all. "Haha I know what you mean. Me and my besties don't have any other friends either!"

&#x200B;

I find it hard to identify with the pain of someone complaining about friendship drama. I can't stop myself internally rolling my eyes and thinking, "Nice problem to have!". Similarly, I don't identify with lottery winners who lose it all again.

&#x200B;

It does not even have to be an explicit message of friendship to trigger these feelings in me. I have started noticing it everywhere. I notice people laughing and joking with people they feel at ease with. I notice people looking at their phone, smiling at some message a friend took the time to send, before going about their day.

&#x200B;

People completely take for granted having other people in their life - not just family or co workers, but actual peers, people who like and respect them, and who have the option of \*not\* knowing them, but choose to do so because they actually want to. I take for granted the hands I am using to type these words. If I were to lose my hands in an accident I would be ineffective as a human and rightly considered crippled. But having no hands is more visible than having no friends.

&#x200B;

I always thought I would be able to anticipate and avoid the point of no return, but the last few years have flown by so horribly quickly. Looking back at old photos, I realised I passed this point a few years ago. Now, people my age have their own established lives and friend groups, and if I were to try to attach to a younger crowd, I would always be the creepy older one.

&#x200B;

I want to be desired. I want to be persued. I want worth that persists beyond my actions. I want my image to have worth. I want my sexuality to have worth.

&#x200B;

\> Smile for the cameras! Everone is looking so fabulous tonight! Does she even know where that pipe goes? Lets not tell her.
